# dlectroflow environment variables.
#
# Local dev — copy this file to `.env` FIRST, then run setup:
#
#   cp .env.example .env   # as-is; the DATABASE_URL below already matches docker/docker-compose.yml
#   npm run setup          # starts Postgres in Docker, installs deps, applies migrations
#
# `.env` has to exist before `npm run setup`, because setup's last step is
# `prisma migrate dev` and that reads DATABASE_URL from `.env`.
#
# WHICH FILE: `.env` vs `.env.local` — the split is the thing that trips people up.
#   `.env`        read by the Prisma CLI (`npm run setup`, `db:migrate`, `db:studio`
#                 — see prisma.config.ts) AND by `next dev` / `next start`.
#                 DATABASE_URL must be here. One `.env` is enough to run everything.
#   `.env.local`  read by Next.js only, and it OVERRIDES `.env`. Prisma never reads
#                 it, so a DATABASE_URL that lives only here breaks migrations.
#
# Both are gitignored — never commit real secrets.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Claude API key (server-side only, never exposed to the browser).
# Get one at https://console.anthropic.com → API keys.
# Required for the DEFAULT provider only: since #59 this key is what the
# `anthropic` provider needs, and it's unused if you point the app at your own
# model instead — see the "LLM provider (BYO-LLM / self-host)" block below.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Database connection string (PostgreSQL). Must live in `.env` — the Prisma CLI
# does not read `.env.local`.
#   Local dev:  the default below IS the local Docker Compose database (user,
#   password and db name all `dlectroflow` — the POSTGRES_* values committed in
#   docker/docker-compose.yml), so `cp .env.example .env` works with no edit. Not a
#   secret: it's the same value that file already publishes, and the container
#   only ever listens on your own machine. `npm run setup` starts it for you.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://dlectroflow:dlectroflow@localhost:5432/dlectroflow?schema=public"
#   Production: injected from GitLab Secrets Manager (see docs/deploy-runbook.md)

# ── Optional / feature-gated ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Reclaim.ai is not connected from inside dlectroflow at all — no token, and no
# in-app OAuth flow (that was removed in #36). Instead you point Reclaim's own
# Google Tasks integration at your account, and Reclaim auto-schedules the
# Google Tasks list this app writes steps into (see the Google Tasks route
# below). Nothing to set here.

# Google Tasks route (recommended for scheduling → Reclaim syncs your Google
# Tasks). Create an OAuth client in Google Cloud Console (enable the Google
# Tasks API; add redirect http://localhost:3000/api/google/oauth/callback).
# You connect at runtime in the app; only the client id/secret live here.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Optional: which Google Tasks list Reclaim syncs (case-insensitive contains).
# NOTE Reclaim syncs EXCLUSIVELY from its own "🗓 Reclaim" list, so pointing this
# elsewhere means Reclaim never sees what the app pushes — which is a legitimate
# setup if you have no Reclaim, and is exactly what SCHEDULING_SYNTAX detects.
# GOOGLE_TASKS_LIST_NAME=reclaim
# Optional: which title syntax scheduled tasks are written in (#104). Detected
# from the list name by default — a list matching "reclaim" gets Reclaim's
# parenthetical parameters, anything else gets a clean title plus Google Tasks'
# native due date. Set to "reclaim" or "plain" to override the detection.
# SCHEDULING_SYNTAX=
# Optional: the timezone the scheduling windows are computed in (working hours
# are Mon–Fri 08:30–18:00 work, evenings + weekends personal). Defaults to
# Europe/London; set an IANA zone if you are self-hosting elsewhere.
# SCHEDULING_TIMEZONE=Europe/London

# End-of-day round-up EMAIL (opt-in) — Resend. The in-app + desktop round-up
# work without this; email is cleanly disabled unless RESEND_API_KEY is set.
# Get a key at https://resend.com → API Keys, then opt in from the dashboard.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# From address (defaults to Resend's onboarding@resend.dev sandbox sender).
# ROUNDUP_FROM_EMAIL="dlectroflow <you@yourdomain.com>"

# ── Owner auth (workspace access) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign-in is invite-only (#35): an OAuth identity only becomes an account if a
# matching row exists in the Allowlist table. Everyone else uses a guest sandbox.
# In production the app refuses to boot if these are unset.

# Deployed origin (e.g. https://dlectroflow.dev), used to pin
# OAuth redirect URIs and the Secure cookie flag so they can't be influenced
# by spoofed Host / X-Forwarded-* headers. Required in production (the Helm
# chart always sets it — see charts/dlectroflow/templates/deployment.yaml);
# leave unset for local dev, where it falls back to the request's forwarded headers.
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=

AUTH_PROVIDER=gitlab
# Comma-separated owner identities (GitLab numeric user id for the gitlab
# provider; a username or an email also match). Seeded into the Allowlist table
# as role="owner" by `npm run seed:allowlist`, which the deploy runs after
# migrations — this is what stops the owner being locked out by their own
# invite-only instance. Matching is case-insensitive.
OWNER_ALLOWLIST=
# Random 32+ byte string, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 48`.
AUTH_SESSION_SECRET=
# GitLab OAuth application (Settings → Applications). Redirect URIs:
#   https://<host>/api/auth/gitlab/callback  and  http://localhost:3000/api/auth/gitlab/callback
# Scope: read_user.
GITLAB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GITLAB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── LLM provider (BYO-LLM / self-host) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Which LLM backend to use. Provider selection is a deploy-time decision (env
# only — there is no in-app provider switcher).
#   anthropic         → the hosted Claude API (default; requires ANTHROPIC_API_KEY).
#   openai-compatible → any OpenAI-compatible endpoint: a local runner
#                       (Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM) or another hosted vendor.
LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic

# The following LLM_* vars apply ONLY when LLM_PROVIDER=openai-compatible.
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint base URL, e.g. http://localhost:11434/v1 (Ollama).
# LLM_BASE_URL=
# API key for that endpoint. Optional for local runners that need none — the
# adapter sends a harmless placeholder when this is unset.
# LLM_API_KEY=
# Model id to use (e.g. llama3.1:8b). Required for openai-compatible.
# LLM_MODEL=
# Optional owner/guest model split; each falls back to LLM_MODEL when unset.
# LLM_OWNER_MODEL=
# LLM_GUEST_MODEL=
# Whether the configured model does native tool-calling (default true). Set to
# "false" for a local model without tool support (structured output falls back
# to JSON-in-text).
# LLM_SUPPORTS_TOOLS=true

# ── Phase 2: guest cost controls + models ───────────────────────────────────
GUEST_AI_QUOTA_PER_WINDOW=5
GUEST_AI_WINDOW_HOURS=24
GUEST_GLOBAL_DAILY_GUEST_CAP=10
GUEST_SANDBOX_TTL_HOURS=24
# These two apply to the `anthropic` provider only; on openai-compatible the
# LLM_OWNER_MODEL / LLM_GUEST_MODEL vars above take over instead.
OWNER_BREAKDOWN_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6
GUEST_BREAKDOWN_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5
# Secret — salts the guest IP hash (never stores the raw IP). >=16 chars.
GUEST_IP_HASH_SALT=CHANGEME-long-random-value

# ── #35 Phase B: per-user AI policy (signed-in accounts) ────────────────────
# The rolling window a signed-in account's AI usage is measured over, in hours.
# Default 720 (30 days). It slides from each person's FIRST breakdown and resets
# when it lapses — it is NOT a calendar month. The per-person quota itself is not
# an env var: the owner sets it per account in Settings → People (User.aiQuota),
# because "who pays for AI" is a per-person decision.
#
# Applies to CAPPED and UNCAPPED accounts alike. Uncapped accounts (including the
# instance owner's own) are metered over this same window but are never refused —
# the count is there so the owner can see consumption in the People panel. Only
# an account with its own LLM key is not counted at all: their key, their bill.
USER_AI_WINDOW_HOURS=720

# AES-256-GCM key for OAuth token columns (#21 P2). 64 hex chars.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
TOKEN_ENC_KEY=

# ── Optional: the streamed lo-fi catalog (#61) ──────────────────────────────
# The focus timer always has music: ten CC0 tracks ship inside the image, one per
# open-lofi category (public/audio/lofi/, provenance in public/audio/LICENSE.md).
# The full open-lofi set is 166 tracks / ~544 MB, which is too much to put in a
# container image, so the rest is read from an object store at run time.
#
# Point this at the directory holding the extracted openlofi.zip — the tracks
# themselves plus the catalog.json manifest beside them, flat, one path segment
# each. The manifest is NOT in the zip: that archive is 166 .mp3 files and
# nothing else, and catalog.json is published separately at
# raw.githubusercontent.com/btahir/open-lofi/main/catalog.json. Any plain HTTP
# file server does: object storage with public reads, nginx, MinIO on the same
# Compose network. The store must allow unauthenticated reads — there is no
# credential to give it — and must honour Range, or tracks play but cannot be
# seeked. http:// is fine for a store on a private network; the browser never
# opens this URL.
#
# It never does, because the app PROXIES the audio rather than linking to it:
# next.config.ts sets `default-src 'self'` with no `media-src`, so the browser
# refuses audio from anywhere but this app, and a focus session — a long,
# unattended, personal page view — makes no third-party request. The bytes are
# fetched server-side and streamed back through /api/focus-catalog/audio, which
# also means any credential the store needs stays on the server.
#
# Unset, unreachable or misconfigured, the player just uses the bundled ten. A
# focus session never starts silent because of this variable.
#
# Setting this also turns on the per-category playlists in Settings (#70), with
# nothing else to configure: a category is offered once the catalog gives it more
# than one track. With only the bundled ten — one per category — there is nothing
# to offer, so that part of the picker is absent rather than greyed out.
# FOCUS_CATALOG_ORIGIN=https://your-store.example.com/openlofi
